{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: archive blob:none compatibility fix at exact head b76a318f30691b5aea96a72a525675c191fe4feb, tree d0c28cb9b4086e0fd2908bbbf3c9b0c7040a8cb0 (review request d29fa286, promise 95861ad9, implementation ac1a5d6f). Landing must wait for the planner's ruling on request 0d838346, as the review request says.\n\nRead order: I read the primary, notes/implementation/2026-10-23-archive-blob-none.md, first and in full. Then I read the six supporting artifacts: packages/git/src/http-read.ts, packages/scope/src/archive-host.ts, their two test files, packages/scope/test/support/archive-artifacts.ts and scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs.\n\nWhat the head changes. The v2 exact-object fetch now sends \"filter blob:none\" in place of \"filter tree:0\", which the host refuses (planner finding da1b3d2f). The bounded full decoder now accepts companion commit and tree objects and bounded deltas. It then returns only the object whose computed ID is the wanted ID, with the type, size and shallow-boundary checks applied to that object. A synchronous reserveObjects(count) callback charges the actual pack object count to the original archive budget before any per-object allocation. The v0 path is unchanged. The installed-archive stand-in now expects blob:none and replies as the host does: the wanted object, every tree below it and, for a commit, the shallow boundary.\n\nWhat I checked myself.\n1. Tree difference. From the gated 4485f196 tree, b76a318f differs only in archive-artifacts.ts, the implementation note and notes/reports/2026-10-23-sprint-report.md. The report is byte-equal to Main de52252f. No file under packages/*/src or apps changed. b76a318f changes only the note compared with the rerun head 5f9048c4 (tree c7233239).\n2. Evidence packets. I checked three packets: every copy is a regular, single-link, mode-0444 file on its own inode, its SHA-256 and size match its row, and no file is unlisted or missing.\n   - Owning packet: manifest baea2d2a, 69 rows, 110,148,632 bytes.\n   - Failed-gate packet: manifest 362ca54c, 44 rows, 36,134,298 bytes.\n   - Continuation packet: manifest 42e3e4f5, 97 rows. Including the manifest, it holds 54,757,598 bytes, as the note says.\n3. Failed gate at 4485f196, from its raw logs. The vitest summary is \"Tests 2 failed | 3500 passed | 3 skipped (3505)\" across 489 files, with no unhandled error. Both failures are in archive-installed.test.ts (lines 861 and 1222). Typecheck, whitespace, commit messages (4 tests pass), helpers and section citations (7 tests pass) all passed. The only guard failure is \"postguard RuntimeError: fixed input changed: mainRefs\" (Main moved to de52252f, which adds only the report). Node 37 and Counting 55 were not reached. The cause is the one in my diagnosis e7ce8c70: the stand-in still required tree:0.\n4. Continuation V3 at 5f9048c4, tree c7233239 (from v3-used-source/checkpoint.json):\n   - vitest.json: the whole archive-installed file passed 6 of 6, including both cases that failed in the gate;\n   - Node block: 37 of 37 passed;\n   - Counting block: 55 of 55 passed.\n   Each child exited 0 with no signal. The earlier V2 attempt stopped before any test body because of a pool-selector error, and the note keeps it on record.\n5. Document checks at b76a318f: git diff --check -B against de52252f is clean. check-commit-messages reports 5 checked, 0 flagged, and its test passes 4 of 4. The section citations, run on an export of the head, report 1,876 resolved, 0 broken and 5 known unresolved, the same as the gate. The citation test passes 7 of 7.\n\nThis evidence meets the three conditions in my assert 5b005182. The tree differs only in the test stand-in, the note and the report. The gate's failures are only the two installed cases and the Main-ref guard, and the rerun covers both installed cases and the two Node blocks the gate never reached. The rerun passes with a stand-in that sends companion trees. The rerun replaces a second whole gate. That goes one step beyond docs/testing.md:600, and the planner has still to accept it in 0d838346. If the planner asks for a whole gate instead, a passing gate at this same tree supports this verdict without another Source review.\n\nArchitecture. The change stays inside the Git HTTP transport and the Scope archive host adapter, and it follows the planner's stated direction. The trusted callback is narrow: it is synchronous, forwarded only on isolated v2 reads, and charges both ledgers of the existing ArchiveBudget, with no refund and no later top-up. One architectural cost remains, recorded as the P2 below. Under blob:none, each commit or tree reply contains every tree below it, so the v2 layout no longer gets the path-local reads it was designed around.\n\nSecurity. I found no defect. The request still names one exact want and is authorized by the same repository credential, with no added ref listing, downgrade, fallback or retry. decodePack checks every object hash, so the wanted object is the one whose computed ID matches. Companions never enter the source cache and give no closure authority; the test that reads the tree later proves this. Tags are refused. The size check uses the caller's limit. Wire bytes, inflated bytes, delta results, object count and the deadline stay bounded by the existing allowance, and actual-count admission comes before allocation. The 400 response body is not read into errors: the test asserts that neither the detail nor the error string contains it. Token redaction is unchanged.\n\nSimplification. The change is small. One decoder path replaces the old exact-one rule for v2, one callback is added, and the selection logic is short. Nothing is duplicated and no dead code is left. The test stand-in is now more faithful, which is better than adding a special case.\n\nFindings.\n- P2 (nonblocking, from assert d2a8427a, kept in the note): every parent read's allowance is still sized for the one wanted object plus 8,192 bytes, and the reply now holds every tree below it. The root tree read therefore refuses once all trees in the archive pass 8,487 bytes. In a local Git reconstruction that happens at 66 passes with no retained files, or at 25 passes with two retained files per pass. After that the Mark stalls again with \"inflated bytes\" and no fallback. The note states this accurately, labels it a reconstruction, and claims no hosted acceptance. The planner owes the stopgap decision, the current Grow pass count and pass frequency, and a tracked follow-up for a parent read whose cost does not grow with the whole archive (0d838346).\n- I found no other finding.\n\nNo hosted acceptance, archive Mark advance or successful whole gate is claimed or approved here. Deployment is planner-owned.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:936fde3d037647a097823d59643afc271c99533a","binding":"assigned","head":"b76a318f30691b5aea96a72a525675c191fe4feb","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:95861ad98a163a0d7b35343c473740c8e82b2b8d\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6c5af37253ce954e41a685af06b55ee72db64eb7\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:163f8852385836a165c0fc7f566110391c828f52","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}